 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np12-01",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Balance",
   "quote": "sic conservatis suis rebus Poenus illusis Cretensibus omnibus",
   "trans": "his property thus saved, the Carthaginian, with all the Cretans fooled",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two ablative absolutes frame the subject, one for his money saved and one for the Cretans fooled, so the trick is summed up neatly before the story moves on.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-02",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "Poenus",
   "trans": "the Carthaginian",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Nepos calls Hannibal \"the Phoenician\", the Roman word that brought to mind proverbial Punic cunning, just after he has tricked the Cretans.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "omnibus",
   "trans": "all",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Not some Cretans but all of them were fooled, which completes the joke of the last chapter.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "eodem animo fuit erga Italiam",
   "trans": "he kept the same attitude towards Italy",
   "line": "",
   "body": "His hatred of Rome is unchanged by exile, as Nepos promised in chapter 1.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-05",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphatic negative",
   "quote": "neque aliud quicquam egit quam",
   "trans": "he did nothing else but",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The negative shows Hannibal's single-minded focus: everything he does at Prusias' court is aimed at Rome.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-06",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "armavit et exercuit",
   "trans": "he armed and trained",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two verbs show Hannibal turning a king into a weapon against Rome, as if Prusias were one of his soldiers.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-07",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "domesticis opibus minus esse robustum",
   "trans": "was not strong enough in his own resources",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Minus, \"less\", gently plays down the king's weakness, but the point is clear: Prusias cannot fight Rome alone.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-08",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "conciliabat ceteros reges, adiungebat bellicosas nationes",
   "trans": "he won over other kings, he joined warlike nations to him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two matching clauses without a connecting word, both in the imperfect, show Hannibal's tireless diplomacy.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Superlative",
   "quote": "Romanis amicissimus",
   "trans": "a very close friend to the Romans",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Eumenes is defined by his friendship with Rome, the opposite of Hannibal, so his conflict with Prusias becomes Hannibal's war with Rome by proxy.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-10",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "et mari et terra",
   "trans": "both by sea and by land",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The war is fought on every front, which raises the stakes.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-11",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "propter Romanorum societatem",
   "trans": "because of his alliance with the Romans",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The reason for Eumenes' advantage comes last: behind every enemy Hannibal faces, Rome is the real power.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-12",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Conditional",
   "quote": "quem si removisset, faciliora sibi cetera fore arbitrabatur",
   "trans": "he thought that if he removed him, everything else would be easier for him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The condition shows Hannibal's clear strategic reasoning: remove the key ally and the rest will follow.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "opprimi",
   "trans": "to be crushed",
   "line": "",
   "body": "A violent verb shows how far Hannibal is prepared to go.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-14",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Gerundive",
   "quote": "ad hunc interficiundum",
   "trans": "for killing him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The blunt gerundive of purpose, with its old-fashioned -und- spelling, states Hannibal's aim without softening: murder.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-15",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Foreshadowing",
   "quote": "talem iniit rationem",
   "trans": "he adopted the following plan",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The phrase echoes capit tale consilium in the Cretan story, so the reader expects another clever trick.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Prusias",
   "quote": "Prusiam",
   "trans": "Prusias",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Prusias I, king of Bithynia (about 228 to 182 BC), in north-west Asia Minor. Nepos says Pontus, loosely: Bithynia lay on the coast of the Black Sea, the Pontus Euxinus.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np12-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Eumenes",
   "quote": "Eumenes",
   "trans": "Eumenes",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Eumenes II, king of Pergamum (197 to 159 BC), Rome's loyal ally in Asia Minor, who gained much of Antiochus' former territory at the peace of Apamea and fought Prusias in the 180s BC.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 12,
 "questions": [
  "How does Nepos show that Hannibal's aims have not changed since chapter 1?",
  "Why does Hannibal decide that Eumenes is the key obstacle, and what does this show about his strategic thinking?",
  "How does Nepos prepare the reader for another trick at the end of this section?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "The kingdoms of Asia Minor",
   "body": "<p>After the peace of Apamea (188 BC) Rome took no land in Asia Minor for itself but rewarded its friends. Eumenes II of Pergamum and the island state of Rhodes received most of the territory Antiochus gave up, and Pergamum became Rome's most important ally in the region.</p><p>Prusias I of Bithynia, in the north-west of Asia Minor on the coast of the Black Sea, was a rival of Pergamum, and the two kings fought over border lands in the 180s BC. For Hannibal, Prusias offered a last chance to strike at Rome through one of its allies.</p>"
  }
 ]
